{"name":"Ulva Lactuca Green","hex":"#90ee90","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ulva","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any organism of the genus Ulva."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ulva"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ulva lactuca","blocks":[{"text":"Ulva lactuca, also known by the common name sea lettuce or broadleaf sea lettuce, is an edible green alga in the family Ulvaceae. It is the type species of the genus Ulva. A synonym is U. fenestrata, referring to its \"windowed\" or \"holed\" appearance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Sea_lettuce_in_Brofjorden_1_-_cropped.jpg/330px-Sea_lettuce_in_Brofjorden_1_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulva_lactuca"},{"title":"Ulva","blocks":[{"text":"Ulva is a small island in the Inner Hebrides of Scotland, off the west coast of Mull. It is separated from Mull by a narrow strait, and connected to the neighbouring island of Gometra by a bridge. Much of the island is formed from Cenozoic basalt rocks, which are formed into columns in places."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Ulva_Boat_Terminal.JPG/330px-Ulva_Boat_Terminal.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulva"},{"title":"Lactuca","blocks":[{"text":"Lactuca, commonly known as lettuce, is a genus of flowering plants in the family Asteraceae. The genus includes at 117 recognized species, distributed worldwide, but mainly in temperate Eurasia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Lactuca_tuberosa_2.jpg/330px-Lactuca_tuberosa_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lactuca"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Ulva_lactuca_-_Sowerby.jpg/330px-Ulva_lactuca_-_Sowerby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Ulva_lactuca_-_Sowerby.jpg/330px-Ulva_lactuca_-_Sowerby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUlva_lactuca_-_Sowerby.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Ulva1.jpg/500px-Ulva1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Ulva1.jpg/500px-Ulva1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tràigh Bhàn, looking South east toward Ben More on Mull.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUlva1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Lochnakealislands.jpg/500px-Lochnakealislands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Lochnakealislands.jpg/500px-Lochnakealislands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A view from Ormaig to the south east: the nearer islets are Geasgill Mòr & Geasgill Beag, while the longer island behind them is Inch Kenneth","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALochnakealislands.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Basaltcliffsulva.jpg/500px-Basaltcliffsulva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Basaltcliffsulva.jpg/500px-Basaltcliffsulva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Basalt cliffs, near Dùn Bhioramuill on south coast","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABasaltcliffsulva.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Mull.topographic.nolabel.jpg/500px-Mull.topographic.nolabel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Mull.topographic.nolabel.jpg/500px-Mull.topographic.nolabel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Topography of Ulva and surrounding area","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A